<exercises xml:id="binomial-approximation-mixed">
    <title>Binomial Approximation to the Hypergeometric Distribution</title>
    
    <p>
        Recall that the binomial distribution is used when we want to calculate the likelihood that an event happens a certain number of times during <m>n</m> independent Bernoulli trials. The binomial distribution can be used to approximate the hypergeometric distribution if the sample size, <m>n</m>, is small relative to the population size <m>N</m> (e.g., the sample is at most 5% of the population, meaning <m>\frac{n}{N} \le 0.05</m>). To understand how and why this works, answer the questions that follow.
    </p>

    <exercise label="ex-fish-hypergeometric">
        <title>Capture-Recapture Scenario [Revisited]</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose there are 1,000 fish in a lake. During the first week, 65 fish are captured, tagged, and released. Then, 30 fish are captured two weeks later. Let <m>X</m> be a hypergeometric random variable representing the number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 30.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <!-- Interactive short answer for calculation -->
        <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            	Let <m>X</m> be a hypergeometric random variable representing the number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 30. What is the probability that 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged? In other words, use the hypergeometric probability distribution to find <m>P(X=3)</m>.  
              Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three places. 
            </p>
            <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.184"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
  
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the hypergeometric formula:
                <me>P(X=3) = \frac{\binom{65}{3}\binom{935}{27}}{\binom{1000}{30}} \approx 0.184</me>
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
</task>

    <task> 
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the capture of each fish to be a trial. If the sample is taken without replacement, then we have 30 trials.  Compute each of the following probabilities. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to four decimal places. Remember - The sample of size 30 is taken <em>without replacement</em>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
        <!-- Part B1: First fish -->
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>What is the probability that the first fish captured is tagged?
                </p> 
                <p>
        <fillin width="8" answer="0.0650"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! 
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
</task>
        <!-- Part B2: Second fish -->
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>Suppose that the first fish was tagged. What is the probability that the second fish captured is also tagged?</p>
                  <p>
          <fillin width="8" answer="0.0641"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! 
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        </task>

        <!-- Part B3: Third fish -->
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>Suppose that the first fish was tagged but the second one was not. What is the probability that the third fish captured is tagged?</p>
                  <p>
         <fillin width="8" answer="0.0641"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! 
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        </task>

        <!-- Part B4: Last fish -->
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>4. Suppose 29 fish have been caught and that two of these were tagged. What is the probability that the last fish captured (number 30 in our sample) is tagged?</p>

                  <p>
           <fillin width="8" answer="0.0649"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! 
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        </task>
    </task>

    <!-- Non-interactive conceptual explanations using essay text boxes -->
   <task>
     <statement correct="yes">
    <p>
      These trials can be considered Bernoulli trials.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Write.
    </p>
  </feedback>

    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain why our random variable <m>X</m> (number of tagged fish found in the sample of size 60) is NOT a binomial random variable. Also tell me, What changes would you have to make to the physical capture-recapture protocol in order for <m>X</m> to be a true binomial random variable?
            </p>
        </statement>
         <response/>
        <solution>
            <p>
                It is not binomial because true binomial distribution requires completely independent trials with a constant probability of success. To make it a true binomial setup, you would have to sample <strong>with replacement</strong>: throw each fish back into the lake immediately after checking it before drawing the next one.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Because the sample size is small relative to the population (<m>30/1000 = 0.03 \lt 0.05</m>), the probabilities you calculated earlier quite similar! It is therefore reasonable to just fix the probability of success at <m>p = \frac{65}{1000} = 0.065</m> for all 30 trials. This would allow us to use the binomial distribution to get an <em>approximation</em> for <m>P(X=3)</m>! 
            </p>
            <p>
                 Re-compute the probability that exactly 3 of the 30 fish will be tagged using the binomial approximation. Write your answer as a decimal rounded to three places. 
                 
                 </p>
                  <p>
                  How does this compare to your initial answer using the hypergeometric distribution in Part A? 
            </p>
         <p>
           <fillin width="8" answer="0.182"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
            <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Using the Binomial formula:
                <me>P(X=3) = \binom{30}{3}(0.065)^3(0.935)^{27} \approx 0.182</me>
                Comparing this to the hypergeometric result (<m>0.184</m>), the binomial approximation is incredibly close and accurate to within two-thousandths.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        </evaluate>
        </evaluation>
        </task>
        </exercise>




<exercise label="ex-cookie-analysis">
        <title>Analyzing the Cookie Problem Approximation</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Refer back to the Brussels-Sprout-with-Turnip-Chip cookie problem. You originally used a hypergeometric framework because you sampled a total of 3 cookies from a small batch of 21 cookies (where 8 were Brussels-Sprout-with-Turnip-Chip flavors).
            </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
              <statement>
                <p>
                 Comment on whether a binomial approximation is mathematically appropriate for that cookie scenario. Justify your reasoning.
                </p>
              </statement>
            
        <solution>
            <p>
                The binomial approximation is <strong>not appropriate</strong> here. Testing the population threshold: <m>\frac{n}{N} = \frac{3}{21} \approx 0.143</m>. Because <m>14.3\% \gt 5\%</m>, the sample size is too large relative to the population. Each draw alters the remaining cookie composition too dramatically to ignore.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Regardless of appropriateness, let's see how much error occurs if we force a binomial calculation anyway. Let the constant probability of picking a Brussels-Sprout cookie be <m>p = \frac{8}{21} \approx 0.38</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                 Use the binomial distribution to compute the probability of getting exactly one Brussels-Sprout-with-Turnip-Chip cookie in your sample of 3 cookies.
            </p>
        </statement>
         <solution>
            <p>
               Using the Binomial formula:P(X=1) = \binom{3}{1}(0.38)^1(0.62)^2 \approx 0.438
            </p>
        </solution>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compare your answers using the hypergeometric and binomial distributions. What do you notice? Why did this occur?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <solution>
            <p>
              The exact hypergeometric result was 0.469. Because the sample size threshold was violated, the approximation is noticeably inaccurate (off by roughly 0.031 or over 3%).
            </p>
          </solution>
        </task>
       
</exercise>







        </exercises>

    


  </section>
